    <body>
      <sec id="article-141285.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation is an intervention that delivers pressurized gas to the lungs&#x000a0;via an external interface, augmenting alveolar ventilation and reducing the work of breathing without endotracheal intubation. Indications include acute and chronic respiratory failure, particularly cases with hypercapnic and cardiogenic etiologies. Contraindications include impaired airway protection, hemodynamic instability, and facial trauma. Advantages over intubation include reduced risk of ventilator-associated complications and preservation of airway defenses. Limitations include mask intolerance, air leak, and the need for close monitoring and titration to ensure effectiveness and prevent deterioration.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in performing noninvasive positive pressure ventilation and evaluating candidates. Participants will advance their mastery of the relevant anatomy, action mechanism, indications, contraindications, preparation requirements, technique, benefits, and limitations of the procedure. Improved skills will equip clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams&#x000a0;caring for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess noninvasive positive pressure ventilation&#x000a0;candidates based on clinical presentation, arterial blood gas abnormalities, and exclusion of conditions requiring immediate intubation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement noninvasive positive pressure ventilation according to evidence-based protocols, ensuring proper initiation and equipment setup, titration, and ongoing assessment of patient response.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine indications for endotracheal intubation in patients receiving noninvasive positive pressure ventilation by assessing for clinical deterioration, ventilatory failure, or intolerance to therapy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply effective strategies to improve care coordination among interprofessional team members to facilitate positive outcomes for patients undergoing&#x000a0;noninvasive positive pressure ventilation.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=141285&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=141285">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Noninvasive ventilation (NIV) was first reported in the mid-18th century by the Scottish physician John Dalziel.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r1">[1]</xref> In 1864, Alfred F. Jones patented the first American tank respirator, known as the iron lung, which represented early noninvasive negative-pressure ventilation. In 1938, Alvan L. Barach et al described a new form of NIV as a treatment for pulmonary edema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r2">[2]</xref> However, Oertel described intermittent positive pressure ventilation (PPV) earlier in 1878.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>During the polio epidemic, a mortality rate exceeding 80% prompted innovation by physicians such as Bjorn Ibsen, an anesthesiologist from Copenhagen, Denmark. PPV was applied in 1952 via tracheotomy but required manual delivery. The approach reduced mortality to nearly 40%.&#x000a0;However, pressure delivery posed logistical challenges due to the absence of&#x000a0;mechanical&#x000a0;PPV equipment, necessitating manual bagging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Over the past century,&#x000a0;PPV has advanced substantially and is now used to treat respiratory failure from multiple etiologies. PPV has demonstrated effectiveness in preventing intubation compared to standard oxygen therapy in acute settings. Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV) encompasses several methods of respiratory support, with bilevel positive airway pressure (BiPAP) as the most commonly used modality.</p>
        <p>The latest guidelines from the American Thoracic Society (ATS) and the European Respiratory Journal (ERJ) support the use of NIPPV in acute exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and in acute respiratory failure (ARF) secondary to cardiogenic pulmonary edema (CPE), where the evidence and strength of recommendation are highest.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r5">[5]</xref> Additional evidence and conditional recommendations support use in other ARF settings, including postoperative states and chest trauma. Several studies also&#x000a0;demonstrate&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;efficacy of NIPPV in chronic respiratory diseases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>This activity examines the physiological principles underlying NIPPV and delineates its indications and contraindications.&#x000a0;Emphasis will also be given to equipment, preparation, and treatment modalities.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Lung compliance is defined as the change in lung volume in response to a change in pressure, represented by dV/dP, where V is lung volume, and P&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;transpulmonary pressure (TPP). TPP is&#x000a0;the difference between alveolar pressure&#x000a0;and intrapleural pressure. TPP represents the pressure that counteracts the inward elastic recoil of the lungs, thereby preventing collapse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;At rest, TPP is slightly positive. During inspiration, diaphragmatic contraction decreases&#x000a0;intrapleural pressure, increasing TPP and resulting in lung expansion. Normal lung tissue follows a compliance curve in which changes in TPP produce corresponding increases in lung volume. Lung pathology may increase or decrease compliance, distorting the curve and requiring higher TPP to achieve a given volume change.</p>
        <p>Emphysema shifts the compliance curve upward, placing TPP in a region where further increases produce minimal additional lung volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r9">[9]</xref> Acute respiratory distress syndrome and CPE reduce compliance due to fluid-filled alveoli exerting mechanical stress on adjacent air-filled alveoli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>NIPPV reduces the work of breathing through 3 distinct mechanisms. Application of positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) via expiratory positive airway pressure (EPAP) enables surpassing the intrinsic PEEP required to initiate inspiration and improves compliance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r11">[11]</xref> Application of inspiratory positive airway pressure (IPAP) augments TPP during inspiration, thereby reducing respiratory effort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>According to the 2020 ATS/ERJ ARF guidelines, NIPPV carries a strong recommendation in the following situations:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>BiPAP&#x000a0;for acute or acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis secondary to COPD exacerbation, with pH at or below 7.35</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>BiPAP to prevent endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation in patients who are not in immediate clinical deterioration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>BiPAP or continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) for CPE</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The ATS/ERJ guidelines provide a conditional recommendation for NIPPV in the following settings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Immunosuppression with&#x000a0;ARF warrants early NIV</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>ARF in the postoperative period</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dyspnea in end-stage conditions, such as terminal cancer, necessitates palliative management</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>ARF&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;chest trauma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postextubation respiratory failure prevention in high-risk patients</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>NIPPV has also demonstrated efficacy in chronic respiratory diseases, including chronic stable COPD with hypercapnia, obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS), obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), respiratory failure secondary to a neuromuscular disorder (NMD), and restrictive thoracic disorders. A recent multicenter, randomized trial demonstrated that preoxygenation with NIPPV significantly reduced the incidence of hypoxemia during emergency tracheal intubation in adults with critical illness compared with preoxygenation using an oxygen mask.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r13">[13]</xref> The NIPPV group also experienced fewer cardiac arrests, with no significant difference in aspiration rates.</p>
        <p>The landmark RENOVATE trial (n = 1,766) demonstrated that high-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO) is noninferior to NIV in preventing intubation or death within 7 days across diverse patient populations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r14">[14]</xref> HFNO provides superior patient comfort compared with NIV, with no significant differences in 28- or 90-day mortality between groups. Noninferiority was not established for immunocompromised patients, for whom NIV may remain the preferred option. Updated indications for HFNO and NIV are outlined in the table below.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-141285.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table.&#x000a0;Updated Clinical Indications of High-Flow Nasal Oxygen and Noninvasive Ventilation by Etiology (2025)</title>
