        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Absolute contraindications to NIV include conditions that prevent safe application or effective ventilation. These conditions include facial trauma or burns, which may impede mask fitting; fixed upper airway obstruction, which prevents airflow; active vomiting, which increases the risk of aspiration; and respiratory or cardiac arrest, in which immediate invasive support is required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Relative contraindications encompass situations in which NIV may be attempted with caution or close monitoring, but the risk of failure or complications is elevated. These conditions include recent facial, upper airway, or upper gastrointestinal surgery; life-threatening hypoxemia;&#x000a0;inability to protect the airway; and medical or hemodynamic instability, such as hypotensive shock, acute myocardial infarction requiring intervention, or uncontrolled ischemia or arrhythmias. Additional considerations are altered mental status or agitation, bowel obstruction, copious respiratory secretions, focal pulmonary consolidation, undrained pneumothorax, and severe comorbidities that may compromise the effectiveness or safety of NIV.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>NIV devices differ according to mode and functionality. CPAP delivers a continuous flow, whereas BiPAP is typically delivered in either spontaneous or spontaneous/timed (S/T) modes. In BiPAP spontaneous mode (BiPAP S), the device augments the patient&#x02019;s own breaths, while BiPAP S/T provides a backup rate slightly below the patient&#x02019;s respiratory rate. Advanced NIPPV devices include additional monitoring features and ventilatory assistance, such as average volume-assured pressure support (AVAPS). AVAPS delivers a target tidal volume using a proprietary algorithm that automatically adjusts inspiratory pressure to maintain optimal ventilation. AVAPS&#x000a0;may be combined with auto-EPAP (AVAPS-AE) to preserve upper airway patency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>In BiPAP S/T mode, IPAP and EPAP are fixed, and pressure support (IPAP minus EPAP) is constant, with a set backup rate. AVAPS provides minimum and maximum pressure limits, adjustable EPAP (auto-EPAP), automatic pressure support to achieve an assured average tidal volume, and continuous monitoring of ventilation parameters. Additional components of the NIV circuit include a mask, tubing, an oxygen supply, a power source, and an optional humidifier. Mask types include nasal masks, oronasal masks, and helmets, each offering distinct advantages and limitations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Initiation and titration of NIPPV should be performed by physicians with experience and formal training in its use and monitoring requirements. Qualified physicians often come from specialties such as internal medicine, anesthesiology, surgery, emergency medicine, pulmonary medicine, and critical care. Support staff for the procedure include personnel proficient in device operation and in managing potential complications, typically respiratory therapists or registered nurses with critical care expertise.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s8" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>NIPPV should be initiated after careful selection of the patient, ventilator, and interface (see <bold>Image</bold>:&#x000a0;Interface Selection and Technical Optimization for Noninvasive Ventilation). Successful implementation largely depends on patient cooperation and requires a thorough explanation of the goals, procedure, and potential complications prior to initiation. Mask fitting should be performed with sufficient time for patient acclimation before securing the interface. The ventilator is then connected to the mask and activated with appropriate oxygen supplementation. Initiation and titration of NIPPV must be conducted under close clinical supervision, with support from nursing and respiratory therapy personnel. Titration protocols vary by institution and underlying pathology, and relatively few standardized protocols are published.&#x000a0;A protocol for NIPPV titration in OSA has been described in the Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r19">[19]</xref> A recent review in the Annals of the American Thoracic Society provides a titration protocol for chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure in COPD.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Titration of Noninvasive Positive Pressure Ventilation in Acute Settings</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinical experience indicates that NIPPV titration in the acute intensive care setting&#x000a0;can be divided into 3 categories. The 1st category includes patients with simple airway conditions, such as OSA, requiring EPAP or CPAP alone. Pressure titration in this context aims to overcome upper airway collapsing pressure, which&#x000a0;may be estimated from actual body weight. A practical starting point for EPAP or CPAP is approximately 10% of the patient&#x02019;s body weight in kilograms.</p>
        <p>The 2nd category includes patients with hypercapnic or mixed respiratory failure, such as acute exacerbation of COPD without upper airway obstruction (no obesity or suspicion of OSA). In this category, clinicians can initiate EPAP at 5 cm H2O and apply high-intensity pressure support of 10&#x000a0;to 15 cm H2O. Pressure support&#x000a0;may be titrated based on the patient&#x02019;s response, as measured by arterial blood gas (ABG) carbon dioxide levels.</p>
        <p>The 3rd category encompasses patients hospitalized with hypercapnic respiratory failure associated with obesity or OSA. This group presents the greatest challenge, requiring careful adjustment of both inspiratory and expiratory pressures to prevent upper airway collapse while maintaining adequate ventilation. Management combines strategies from the first 2 categories: EPAP is titrated to overcome upper airway collapsing pressure using the 10% rule of actual body weight in kilograms, and high-intensity pressure support of 10&#x000a0;to 15 cm H2O is applied to ensure adequate ventilation. ABG results guide titration.</p>
        <p>Once the pH is normalized, pressure support can be weaned gradually to allow a slight increase in hypercapnia, which&#x000a0;may be managed in an outpatient setting. Patients with chronic respiratory failure suspected of having OHS should be discharged on NIPPV until outpatient diagnostic polysomnography and laboratory positive&#x000a0;airway pressure (PAP) titration are completed. BiPAP S/T mode&#x000a0;may be applied in the interim using empiric settings or autotitrating NIPPV modes, such as&#x000a0;volume-assured pressure support (VAPS) with auto-EPAP (VAPS-AE), which adjusts EPAP to maintain upper airway patency in patients with OSA.</p>
        <p>Monitoring in the acute setting requires evaluation of both subjective and physiologic patient responses. Caution is necessary when obtaining patient-reported respiratory status, as some individuals may minimize or deny discomfort, confounding clinical assessment. Patients likely to succeed with NIPPV exhibit a reduction in respiratory rate within 1 to 2 hours, decreased heart rate, and improved ventilator synchrony.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r21">[21]</xref> ABG analysis should be performed 1 hour after BiPAP initiation, as decreases in&#x000a0;arterial carbon dioxide pressure (PaCO<sub>2</sub>) and increases in pH predict treatment success.</p>
        <p>Tidal volume and air leak require close monitoring, as uncorrected abnormalities can contribute to NIPPV failure. Hemodynamic parameters, including blood pressure and heart rate, must also be observed because increased intrathoracic pressure can elevate right ventricular afterload and reduce right ventricular preload. These effects are consistent with prior studies demonstrating reduced cardiac output after NIPPV initiation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Titration of N<bold>oninvasive Positive Pressure Ventilation</bold> in Chronic Settings</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with COPD and chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure, NIPPV is indicated for those with stable hypercapnia (PaCO2 &#x02265; 52 mm Hg). Prospective randomized controlled trials have demonstrated significant improvements in survival and clinical outcomes, including reduced exacerbation rates and better disease control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r25">[25]</xref> The clinical benefits of NIPPV in stable COPD appear to result from high-intensity pressure support aimed at normalizing PaCO2 or at least achieving a 20% reduction in&#x000a0;carbon dioxide levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Experience with stable patients with COPD without evidence of OSA or obesity supports the use of BiPAP S/T mode with IPAP of 15 to 20 cm H2O. In patients with suspected OSA or obesity (OSA&#x02013;COPD overlap), EPAP must be adjusted to overcome upper airway opening pressure. This&#x000a0;value&#x000a0;may be determined from PAP titration during a sleep study when obstructive apneas are eliminated. If sleep study data are unavailable, EPAP may be empirically set using the 10% of body weight rule. For example, a patient weighing 70 kg would receive EPAP of 7 cm H2O.</p>
        <p>OHS is defined by the combination of obesity (body mass index &#x02265; 30 kg/m2), daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 &#x02265; 45 mm Hg), and the presence of OSA (apnea&#x02013;hypopnea index or AHI &#x02265; 5 events/hour) after exclusion of other causes of alveolar hypoventilation. First-line treatment for OHS with coexistent OSA is CPAP. When CPAP is not tolerated or fails to correct ventilation (PaCO2 &#x02265; 45 mm Hg), NIPPV using BiPAP S/T mode or VAPS is recommended (see prescription parameters below).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Pressure selection in OHS associated with OSA requires elimination of apneas and hypopneas (AHI &#x0003c; 5 events/hour) while correcting hypoventilation and sustained hypoxia. Dedicated pressure titration in a sleep laboratory is recommended, with follow-up in the clinic to assess ABG and adherence to device therapy, typically within 4&#x000a0;to 6 weeks.</p>
        <p>Thoracic restrictive disorder with hypercapnic respiratory failure is defined by ventilatory defects associated with elevated PaCO2 (&#x02265;45 mm Hg) due to NMDs, including amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, spinal cord injury, muscular dystrophy, diaphragm paralysis, spina bifida, and congenital central hypoventilation syndrome, as well as chest wall deformities or other ventilatory drive defects, in the absence of interstitial lung disease. NIPPV in thoracic restrictive disorder follows principles similar to those used in other hypoventilation syndromes. Careful assessment for coexistent OSA and monitoring of PaCO2 and oxygenation during wakefulness and sleep are essential after therapy initiation. BiPAP S/T mode or VAPS&#x000a0;may be applied empirically in this condition, without prior sleep testing.</p>
        <p>Prescribing physicians may use the following guide to complete AVAPS-AE settings based on clinical assessment and diagnostic findings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Primary mode: AVAPS-AE with target tidal volume</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tidal volume (8 mL/kg): _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maximum pressure: _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maximum pressure support: _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Minimum pressure support: _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maximum EPAP: _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Minimum EPAP: _____</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rate: _____&#x000a0;(if set to &#x0201c;auto,&#x0201d; then &#x0201c;not applicable&#x0201d;)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The&#x000a0;2021&#x000a0;American College of Chest Physicians Outcomes and Network for Monitoring Access to Positive-Pressure Ventilation Technical Expert Panel (CHEST ONMAP TEP) report addressed Medicare coverage barriers for home NIV across 5 conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r28">[28]</xref> Key recommendations for hypoventilation syndromes identified 6 issues related to NIPPV use, as follows (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Barriers and Proposed Solutions for Noninvasive Ventilation Access):</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Excessive focus on ABGs:&#x000a0;Venous blood gas (VBG) or PaCO2 monitoring&#x000a0;may be used instead.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Testing on supplemental oxygen: PaCO2 measurement should not require removal of oxygen therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sleep study requirement:&#x000a0;Hospital discharge should not mandate a sleep study for OSA.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spirometry requirement:&#x000a0;Spirometry should not be required for NIV qualification.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>BiPAP S&#x000a0;mode failure requirement: Patients should not be required to fail BiPAP S before initiation of BiPAP S/T mode.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hospital discharge: Qualifying patients should be enabled for home NIV at discharge.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Implementation of these recommendations may streamline access to home NIV and reduce unnecessary testing or delays. Adoption of&#x000a0;these guidelines can improve patient-centered care for individuals with hypoventilation syndromes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s9" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The most common complication of NIPPV is mask discomfort. More serious adverse effects include skin rash secondary to hypersensitivity or infection and, rarely, nasal bridge ulcers. Aerophagia and sialorrhea may also occur. Pressure-related symptoms include discomfort, ear pain, sinus pain, and gastric insufflation. Serious pressure-related complications include pneumothorax, pneumocephalus, and pneumomediastinum associated with NIPPV in the setting of SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) pneumonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r30">[30]</xref> Aspiration is a major complication, and careful patient selection is required to minimize aspiration risk during NIPPV use. Sedation during NIPPV remains poorly studied because of concerns regarding the increased risk of aspiration and hypoventilation. In patients with concurrent respiratory failure and compromised cardiac output, NIPPV may rarely result in hemodynamic compromise due to increased intrathoracic pressure, elevated right ventricular afterload, and reduced preload.</p>
        <p>The final complication involves patient&#x02013;ventilator dyssynchrony with BiPAP. Dyssynchrony&#x000a0;manifests&#x000a0;as either failure to trigger or failure to cycle, resulting in inadequate gas exchange and increased expiratory muscle use. Failure to cycle may occur in COPD, where rapid inhalation does not allow sufficient time for the ventilator to transition from inspiration to expiration. Expiration against ongoing inspiratory pressure delivery results in respiratory discomfort and distress. Failure to trigger may occur in NMDs such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, in which insufficient negative inspiratory pressure prevents initiation of assisted breaths.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Respiratory failure is a life-threatening condition responsible for approximately 30% of in-hospital mortality. Definitive management involves endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation.&#x000a0;However, this intervention is associated with significant morbidity and mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r32">[32]</xref> Complications of intubation include laryngospasm, bronchospasm, incorrect tube placement, aspiration, and hypotension. Complications of prolonged mechanical ventilation include ventilator-induced lung injury, ventilator-associated pneumonia, and gastrointestinal complications, including peptic ulcer disease and colonization of the gastrointestinal tract by aerobic gram-negative bacteria. NIPPV has been shown to reduce intubation rates in COPD, CPE, and pneumonia, and to decrease the need for reintubation in hypercapnic respiratory failure following extubation. NIPPV is a resource-intensive intervention that requires substantial time and should be performed under the supervision of clinicians and support staff with appropriate expertise.</p>
        <p>Current guidelines emphasize early identification of NIV failure to prevent delayed intubation and patient self-inflicted lung injury. The ROX index, calculated as peripheral oxygen saturation (SpO2) divided by the fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO2), then divided by respiratory rate (SpO2/FiO2 &#x000f7; respiratory rate), is used to monitor HFNO, with a value below 4.88 at 12 hours indicating a high likelihood of failure. Risk factors for NIV failure include PaO2/FiO2 less than 150 in acute respiratory distress syndrome, tidal volumes exceeding 9&#x000a0;to 9.5 mL/kg, and lack of clinical improvement within 1 to 2 hours.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141285.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Important points to remember when considering NIV include the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Therapy should be initiated as early as possible.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arterial pH below 7.35 with elevated PaCO2 indicates acute or chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure requiring intensive care unit admission.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Correction of arterial pH should be established as the initial management goal in acute or chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High-intensity pressure support should be applied in chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure with PaCO2&#x0003e;51 mm Hg and should be targeted toward normalization of carbon dioxide or at least a 20% reduction from baseline.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>EPAP should be adjusted empirically using the 10% rule based on actual body weight in suspected OSA without prior PAP titration.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Timely initiation of NIV is critical to correcting hypercapnia and preventing further respiratory compromise. Escalation to endotracheal intubation should be considered promptly if arterial pH fails to improve or&#x000a0;carbon dioxide&#x000a0;reduction targets are not met.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s11" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The physician initiates NIPPV but requires an interprofessional team approach to ensure successful implementation. The physician performs initial settings and titration, while subsequent patient monitoring is conducted by nurses with critical care training and respiratory therapists. Continuous assessment of mental status and vital signs is essential, and any abnormalities should be promptly communicated to the physician. Intravenous access should be secured if hypotension develops prior to NIPPV initiation. Frequent ABG or VBG measurements are required during titration. Prior NIPPV settings and adjustments must be communicated during care transitions to maintain optimal therapy effectiveness.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141285.s12">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
