      <floats-group id="article-141285.s13">
        <fig id="article-141285.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Interface Selection and Technical Optimization for Noninvasive Ventilation. The illustration outlines clinical recommendations for respiratory support interfaces based on specific patient objectives. High-flow nasal oxygen serves as a bridge between sessions to sustain oxygenation, while helmet and facemask interfaces address varying degrees of respiratory failure. Standard pressure settings for bilevel positive airway pressure include an inspiratory positive airway pressure of 12&#x000a0;to 16 cm H2O and an expiratory positive airway pressure of 4 to 8 cm H2O. Contributed by Abdulghani Sankari, MD, Created with&#x000a0;Copilot Imagecreate</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Interface__Selection__and__Technical__Optimization" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-141285.s14">
        <fig id="article-141285.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Barriers and Proposed Solutions for Noninvasive Ventilation Access.&#x000a0;This infographic identifies 6 critical barriers to noninvasive ventilation and provides expert panel recommendations for policy modification. Document highlights the shift toward using end-tidal partial pressure of carbon dioxide and other noninvasive markers to confirm hypoventilation. Standardized requirements for sleep studies and specific pulmonary function tests are advised for removal to reduce delays in managing chronic respiratory conditions.&#x000a0; Contributed by Abdulghani Sankari, MD&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Designer__x1x" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
